Text SMS 02: Run SMS Opt-Out Members Report

4.	The main purpose of this step is to identify members who have unsubscribed from the SMS program. These members must be removed from future campaign member list, so this is an important compliance step.
5.	We run a Python script that first authenticates with the SMS Manager API using OAuth. The script then retrieves subscription records with an unsubscribed status for the required program.
6.	To avoid processing the same data again, it uses a delta date range and selects only the unsubscribe records added since the previous run.
7.	For each member, it also checks the outbound-message history to capture the most recent applicable campaign template. Confirmation and welcome templates are excluded.
8.	The API results are then matched with the campaign member file using GMPI ID, and the final report includes the member information, unsubscribe details, program ID, phone ID, and last template ID.
9.	The output is saved as a dated CSV file, and a separate execution log is created for monitoring and troubleshooting.”
10.	“Once this report is generated and validated, the next step is SMS Text 02.1, where the opt-out records are loaded into the SMS Opt-Out table.”
Slide change Text SMS 02: Load SMS Opt-Out Members Report
The next step is SMS Text 02.1, which loads the newly identified opt-out members into the SMS Opt-Out table.
The workflow first reads the latest SMS Opt-Out Report CSV from the shared input directory. It also captures the current user story, program name, and report month through the manual-entry section. This makes sure we can change these values for each report month and workflow can be reused on a monthly basis
Next, the workflow dynamically queries the Member Report Month data for the selected reporting month. It retrieves active member information, including GMPI ID, MBI, birth date, insurance company, contract information, and report month.
The opt-out report is joined with the MRM data using GMPI ID. This enriches the API results with the required business and member attributes.
The workflow then converts the unsubscribe and phone activity timestamps from UTC to Central Time. It also captures the source file name and file-creation
The data is cleansed by converting text-based NULL values and empty values into proper database nulls. Only the required fields are retained, and the results are sorted by the opt-out update date.
Before loading, the workflow checks the existing Snowflake table to confirm that the same source file has not already been processed. This prevents duplicate loads.
If the file is new, the validated records are appended to the SMS Opt-Out table in Snowflake.
After the load, the QA process compares the workflow record count with the target-table record count. Once the counts match, the input report is archived.
The final output of this step is an updated SMS Opt-Out table containing the newly unsubscribed members, along with complete source-file and processing information.”
